{:title
 "Emoticons as Delimiters, How Smile's Faces Became the Syntax of a Prompt Language",
 :description
 "How the Smile Prompt Language came to open and close its sections with emoticons, from a 2023 request to emojify a prompt's structure, through token counts and rigidity in 2025, to mouths, eyes, noses, hats and scarves in 2026, in my own words with every source.",
 :keywords
 "emoticon delimiters, prompt delimiters, Smile prompt language syntax, XML vs emoticon delimiters, prompt section markers, token efficient delimiters, mouths eyes noses hats scarves, history of Smile",
 :lede
 "A delimiter marks where a section of a prompt begins and ends. In Smile the delimiters are faces, and from 2025 to 2026 each part of a face came to mean something, the mouth the kind of section, the eyes how strictly it is meant, the size how much attention it asks for.",
 :paras
 ["In THE MAP a delimiter is a face made of parts, mouth, nose, eyes, hat and scarf, each dialled as a character and a count. A pair is one smile met from both sides and stored once, every part is addressable alone, and mirroring is said of a position, so one face gathered twice opens one way and closes the other. The 2023 asks for symbols and emoji that draw a model's attention came before this and are not it, followed in [[t:precursor-a-universal-language-of-prompting]]."
 "The first meaning of a Smile face, in July 2025, was a marker that a section begins, chosen because it is one token. On 3 July I wrote on Discord “It’s called Smile, and uses emojis to indicate sections”, and “(: = 1 token and is composed of things telling the AI something comes next”.[[m:2025-07-03]] On a recording that day the measure was set, “i want it to be one token per thing”.[[m:2025-07-03#2]] Two days later the language guidelines made sure the faces could be typed on a phone, and drew one firm line, “All sad faces are not smiles and cannot be used under any circumstances”.[[m:2025-07-05]]"
 "Within days a second meaning joined it, the face as a pair that opens and closes, and closing became a matter of how much a reader needs. The Prompt Doctor's De-GPT document of 8 July gave the rule of closing, “We always want to complete the smile and close the brackets.”,[[m:2025-07-08]] and named the balance every later version weighs, “This is the trade-off between token efficiency and human + Large Language Model understanding.”[[m:2025-07-08#2]] On 10 July I checked a model's tokenizer myself and found that “it's only capital first letter smile like as a brand name that is one token consistently”.[[m:2025-07-10#2]] By the end of the month the faces had their warmth written into them, “An open Smile face `(:` invites meaning; a closing Smile face `:)` blesses completion.”[[m:2025-07-30#2]]"
 "The third meaning grew through the second half of 2025, the parts of a face saying something themselves, the eyes how strictly an instruction is meant. Closing, meanwhile, was never made a requirement. In August 2025 I wrote that “start markers and end markers are semiotic indicators designed to improve the intelligence of model responses”, and that often a minimal amount of Smile in key places works best.[[m:2025-08-09#3]] In October the parts of a face began to carry meaning, “With a variety of eyes : ; = and mouths { [ ( you get to express 3x3 variations of expressions”,[[m:2025-10-14#2]] and in November the eyes took rigidity, “Emoticon type varies according to Level of rigidity/strictness in instruction.”[[m:2025-11-05]] The comparison with other formats was made the same month, “XML does not have optional end tags, Markdown doesn’t have a natural end section at all”.[[m:2025-11-13]]"
 "The fourth meaning, in spring 2026, gave each face three readings at once, mouth for the kind of section, eyes for strictness, size for attention. In April a prompt set the rule in its own words, “In this prompt, (: and :) mark suggestions you may follow or ignore. (-: and :-) mark rules you must follow exactly.”[[m:2026-04-05#3]] A week later I found two gradients in every face, “the strictness of the instruction with the eyes and the amount of attention needed with the size of the face.”[[m:2026-04-14]] On 21 May the Smile Lang document put three of its readings in three lines, “Mouth = section type”, “Eyes = strictness” and “Bigger face = even more attention”,[[m:2026-05-21#2]] and said why faces at all, “This encodes positivity into the syntax and doesn’t have any baked in expectations”.[[m:2026-05-21]]"
 "The fifth meaning, in summer 2026, made the face an anatomy, parts stored separately and composed, with importance carried by hats and scarves that need not match. On 31 July, “the hat is what is on top of their eyes” and “the scarf is what is under their necks”.[[m:2026-07-31]] In August I took the hash out of the language for its crossover with Markdown, asked that hats and scarves need not match, so “you dont automatically shout at the model four times”,[[m:2026-08-05]] and set five depths of face as the default way to write.[[m:2026-08-07]] On 15 August a face with nothing inside became a gift, a way “to send pure kindness to the model, without any content”.[[m:2026-08-15]] The next day I gave Smile its orientation, “From the front it begins with the mouth and holds inward”, and the one rule of matching, “brackets are the only thing that need to match by default, because they are also the only thing that is essential for a Smile.”[[m:2026-08-16]]"
 "The sixth meaning, from late August 2026, carried faces across a conversation, opened in one turn and closed in another, and made them the default syntax in place of colons. “I want faces to be the syntax default not colons”,[[m:2026-08-23]] and in September every name tag opens with a round face and a square face, where “all round faces are closed by the next prompt by the user” and “all square faces are closed by the model at the end of their response.”[[m:2026-09-05]] Across {{moments}} moments and {{quotes}} quotes, from {{first}} to {{last}}, the face moved from a one-token section marker, to an open and close pair, to a carrier of strictness, to three readings at once, to an anatomy of parts, to a pair held open across turns. The next post, [[t:archetypal-smile]], follows what those faces were always reaching toward."],
 :faq
 [["What are Smile's delimiters?"
 "Emoticons that open and close each section of a prompt, such as (: to open and :) to close. In THE MAP's design the mouth gives the kind of section, the eyes how strictly it is meant, and a bigger face asks for more attention, much of it still part built."]
 ["Why use emoticons as delimiters instead of XML?"
 "XML brings its own expectations of strict pairing and data, and Markdown has no natural end to a section. Emoticons let a prompt choose how many tokens mark a boundary, carry positivity in the syntax, and let the model learn their meaning from the prompt itself."]
 ["Must every Smile face be closed?"
 "Brackets are the only thing that need to match by default. Hats and scarves may be left unmatched, and end markers are offered to improve understanding rather than required."]
 ["What are hats and scarves in Smile?"
 "Exclamation marks worn as a hat, on top of the eyes, or as a scarf, under the neck, to give a face more importance and attention."]]}
